  "insider_note": "Name the theory in the first line — \"Levitt's potassium ion pump theory\" — and then give the ion sequence. The name is quick to write and sets up the rest of the answer.",
  "answer": {
    "page_header": [
      "Section B — Short Answer Question",
      "Transport in Plants (Botany-II) · 4 marks"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_structure",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Structure of the stoma",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Structure",
        "margin_note": "Pore, guard cells, the two wall thicknesses, subsidiary cells. The wall difference is the mark.",
        "lines": [
          "A stoma is a pore in the leaf epidermis.",
          "It is bounded by two GUARD CELLS.",
          "In dicots the guard cells are bean shaped; in",
          "monocots they are dumb-bell shaped.",
          "The wall facing the pore (the inner wall) is",
          "THICK and inelastic.",
          "The outer wall is THIN and elastic.",
          "Guard cells contain chloroplasts.",
          "Around them lie the subsidiary cells."
        ],
        "why": "The two walls differ in thickness, and that difference alone decides that a swollen guard cell must bow outward and open the pore rather than swell evenly.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying both walls are the same. The inner wall is THICK, the outer wall is THIN.",
          "Leaving out the guard cells. The pore is defined by the pair that surrounds it."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Thick inside, thin outside: the thin side stretches, so the pair bends apart."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_opening",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Opening: potassium moves in",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Opening",
        "margin_note": "Name the theory, then the ion order. Do not let water move before the ions.",
        "lines": [
          "Levitt's potassium ion pump theory explains",
          "the mechanism.",
          "In light, K+ ions move INTO the guard cells",
          "from the subsidiary cells.",
          "H+ ions are pumped OUT, so the pH of the guard",
          "cells RISES.",
          "Cl- ions enter passively along with K+.",
          "The water potential of the guard cells falls.",
          "Water enters and the guard cells become turgid.",
          "The thin outer walls stretch, the pair bows",
          "apart, and the pore OPENS."
        ],
        "why": "Ions come in first and water follows them, so the order of the answer has to be ions, then water potential, then water, then shape.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying water enters first. The IONS move first and the water follows them.",
          "Writing that the pH falls during opening. Protons leave, so the pH RISES."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Potassium in, protons out, water follows, pore opens."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_closing",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Closing: potassium moves out",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Closing",
        "margin_note": "The reverse of the opening step, in the same order. Six lines.",
        "lines": [
          "At night, in the absence of light, K+ and Cl-",
          "ions move OUT of the guard cells.",
          "The water potential of the guard cells rises.",
          "Water moves out and the guard cells lose",
          "turgor.",
          "The pair straightens and the pore CLOSES.",
          "The pH of the guard cells falls."
        ],
        "why": "Closing is the same sequence run backwards, so it is checked by whether every arrow in the opening step has been reversed.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Describing closing as a separate mechanism. It is the reverse of opening.",
          "Forgetting the pH. It falls on closing, having risen on opening."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Run every arrow backwards and the pore shuts."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_stress",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Water stress and succulents",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "ABA, succulents",
        "margin_note": "Two special cases: ABA under stress, and the reversed succulent pattern.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "ABA shuts the pore under water stress",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "Under water stress, abscisic acid (ABA) drives",
          "K+ ions OUT of the guard cells, so the stomata",
          "close.",
          "In succulent plants the pattern is reversed.",
          "Organic acids collect at night and make the",
          "guard cells turgid, so the stomata open at",
          "NIGHT and close by day."
        ],
        "why": "ABA and the succulents are the two cases where the light rule does not hold, so they are what the last mark tests.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Leaving out ABA. It is the named hormone for water stress and a whole mark.",
          "Saying succulents open by day. They open at NIGHT, which is how they save water."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Dry day shuts the pore; a succulent opens it at night instead."
      },
